Parvovirus B19 infection
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Classic 'slapped cheek' facial erythema, Lacy reticular rash on extremities, Sudden symmetrical polyarthropathy in adults, Transient aplastic crisis in haemolytic anaemias

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-033
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe, and distinct symmetrical joint stiffness and pain in an adult female, accompanied by a rash, requiring urgent assessment for acute inflammatory polyarthropathy or autoimmune crisis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an adult female experiencing acute, severe symmetrical joint pain in the hands and knees rendering her unable to perform basic tasks, accompanied by a spreading rash. Urgent physical assessment is required to exclude acute septic arthritis, acute rheumatic fever, or an evolving autoimmune flare prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hands and wrists are completely stiff, I can't even hold a pen, and my skin is breaking out in a strange lace pattern."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 5 days ago with a low-grade fever, mild systemic malaise, and coryzal symptoms. These prodromal symptoms resolved 2 days ago, followed immediately by the sudden onset of severe, distinct symmetrical polyarthropathy affecting the metacarpophalangeal joints, wrists, and knees. Yesterday, the patient noted a lacy, reticular, and distinct erythematous maculopapular rash developing on her forearms and thighs. She notes her 5-year-old child had very red cheeks ("slapped cheek" appearance) last week. The rash worsens upon exposure to hot showers.
Functional Status: Ambulatory but severely restricted in fine motor skills due to hand stiffness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living independently; currently unable to perform fine motor tasks like cooking or opening medication bottles.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/80 | HR: 85 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Alert, visibly uncomfortable when moving hands, applying heat packs to wrists.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals marked bilateral, symmetrical tenderness and stiffness in the MCPs, PIPs, wrists, and knees without gross effusions or intense erythema. Skin exam reveals a distinct lacy, reticular erythematous rash over the extensor surfaces of the upper and lower limbs. Facial erythema is absent. Complete absence of Koplik spots or generalized lymphadenopathy. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Classic 'slapped cheek' facial erythema (noted in child, not patient), Lacy reticular rash on extremities, Sudden symmetrical polyarthropathy in adults, and Transient aplastic crisis in haemolytic anaemias (patient has no known haemolytic disease).
Environmental Observations: Patient unable to open standard pill bottles; advised to use a pill organizer filled by a neighbor.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Not pregnant.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Parvovirus B19 infection (Adult presentation) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Rheumatoid Arthritis (new onset) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of the classic lacy reticular rash and a clear epidemiological link to a pediatric Parvovirus infection.

Rubella - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of distinct suboccipital or postauricular lymphadenopathy and the specific reticular nature of the rash.

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, polyarticular involvement without a solitary, hot, grossly swollen joint or high fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severely debilitating symmetrical polyarthropathy. In an adult female, this presentation rapidly raises suspicion for an acute systemic autoimmune flare (e.g., lupus, rheumatoid arthritis) or, less likely, disseminated gonococcal infection. A timely physical examination was essential to differentiate these acute destructive joint pathologies from a benign viral arthropathy (Parvovirus B19), rule out joint effusions requiring aspiration, and provide immediate pharmacological management for her debilitating functional impairment. This intervention satisfied the criteria for an urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600) as it could not safely wait.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe scheduled regular NSAIDs (e.g., naproxen 500mg twice daily with food) for severe joint pain and inflammation. Advise absolute avoidance of intense sunlight or heat to prevent rash exacerbation. Recommend elective serology (anti-Parvovirus B19 IgM) via regular GP if confirmation is desired or if symptoms persist.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the NSAID rigidly on a 12-hour schedule rather than waiting for the pain to peak, the patient will maintain steady-state anti-inflammatory levels; this will reward her with sustained functional use of her hands throughout the day, reinforcing compliance with the dosing schedule.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Easy-open containers for necessities.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2-3 weeks if joint symptoms have not spontaneously resolved.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops extreme sudden fatigue, severe shortness of breath, and extreme pallor (signs of an aplastic crisis), or if a single joint becomes intensely red, hot, swollen, and excruciatingly painful.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on clinical epidemiology; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The rash may wax and wane for several weeks, often exacerbated by heat. The severe arthropathy will completely resolve typically within 2-4 weeks without any permanent joint destruction.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Parvovirus is highly dangerous to pregnant women. You must strictly avoid contact with any pregnant women until your symptoms have completely cleared.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: In adults, this childhood virus attacks the joints rather than just the skin. The joint pain is fierce but entirely temporary and will not cause permanent arthritis damage.
Actionable Steps: Take the anti-inflammatory medication with food strictly twice a day. Keep your skin cool and avoid hot baths, as heat directly flares the rash.

